---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'What does a red LOS light mean on my PTCL router?',
    a: 'LOS means Loss of Signal: the fibre box (ONT) is receiving no light from PTCL\'s equipment. It is a fibre-line fault, not a Wi-Fi problem, and restarting the router will not fix it. Reseat the green fibre connector until it clicks and power-cycle the ONT for 30 seconds; if LOS is still red, call PTCL on 1218 — the fault is in the fibre cable or PTCL\'s equipment.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why is my PTCL DSL light blinking?',
    a: 'A blinking DSL light means the modem is trying to sync with the PTCL exchange. Give it a few minutes after a restart — if it keeps blinking, check the phone cable and splitter, confirm the landline has a dial tone, and restart the modem. A DSL light that never goes solid usually means a line fault; call PTCL on 1218.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What do the lights on a PTCL Flash Fiber ONT mean?',
    a: 'PON solid green means the fibre link is registered and healthy. PON blinking green means it is trying to register. LOS red means no optical signal is arriving. LAN blinking is normal traffic to your router; LAN off means the cable or router port is dead. POWER off means no power at all — check the plug and adapter.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is it safe to unplug the fibre cable from the ONT?',
    a: 'Yes, to reseat it — push the green connector in until it clicks. But never look into the end of a fibre cable or an open ONT port: the light is infrared and invisible, and it can damage your eye before you notice anything. Don\'t bend the thin fibre cable sharply, and don\'t squash it behind furniture.',
  },
];

const faqSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: f.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a },
  })),
};

const breadcrumbSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: 'https://ptcl-speed-test.pages.dev/' },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Guides', item: 'https://ptcl-speed-test.pages.dev/guides' },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: 'PTCL Router Lights Explained', item: 'https://ptcl-speed-test.pages.dev/guides/ptcl-router-lights' },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="PTCL Router Lights Explained — DSL Blinking, LOS Red, PON (2026)"
  description="What do the lights on your PTCL router mean? DSL blinking, red LOS light, PON, Internet and LAN — what each one says about your line and what to do about it."
  canonical="/guides/ptcl-router-lights"
  schema={[faqSchema, breadcrumbSchema]}
>
  <section class="page-head">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> / <a href="/guides">Guides</a> / PTCL Router Lights</p>
      <h1>PTCL Router Lights Explained</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        The small lights on your PTCL modem or fibre box name the fault for you. Read
        them before you call support — and before you restart anything — so you know
        whether the problem is your line, your router, or your Wi-Fi.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container" style="max-width: 720px;">
      <h2>PTCL DSL modem lights</h2>
      <p>
        On a DSL modem (the box connected to your telephone line), the two lights that
        matter most are DSL and Internet.
      </p>
      <table class="data-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Light</th><th>State</th><th>What it means</th><th>What to do</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>DSL</strong></td><td>Solid green</td><td>Line is synced with the exchange — healthy</td><td>Nothing; the line is fine</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>DSL</strong></td><td>Blinking</td><td>Modem is trying to sync</td><td>Wait a few minutes; if it never goes solid, check cables and restart</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>DSL</strong></td><td>Red or off</td><td>Cannot sync — line fault</td><td>Check the phone cable and splitter, confirm dial tone, call 1218</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Internet</strong></td><td>Solid green</td><td>Connected to the internet</td><td>Nothing</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Internet</strong></td><td>Red or blinking</td><td>Line synced but login failed, or no internet</td><td>Restart the modem; if it persists, the login or line needs PTCL</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>WiFi / WLAN</strong></td><td>Off</td><td>Wi-Fi is switched off</td><td>Press the Wi-Fi button on the modem or enable it in settings</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>LAN</strong></td><td>Off</td><td>No device connected by cable</td><td>Check the Ethernet cable and the device</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        If the DSL light never goes solid, pick up the landline handset and listen: no
        dial tone, or heavy crackling, confirms a line fault rather than a modem problem.
        Report it to PTCL on <strong>1218</strong> and mention the DSL light and the dial
        tone — it gets you to a line technician faster.
      </p>

      <h2>PTCL Flash Fiber ONT lights</h2>
      <p>
        On fibre, the thin fibre cable goes into a small box called an ONT (often Huawei,
        ZTE or Nokia). Its lights tell you about the fibre link, not your Wi-Fi.
      </p>
      <table class="data-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Light</th><th>State</th><th>What it means</th><th>What to do</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>POWER</strong></td><td>Off</td><td>No power</td><td>Check the plug and power adapter</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>PON</strong></td><td>Solid green</td><td>Fibre link registered — healthy</td><td>Nothing</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>PON</strong></td><td>Blinking green</td><td>Trying to register on the network</td><td>Wait; if it never settles, it's a provisioning issue — call 1218</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>LOS</strong></td><td>Off</td><td>Optical signal arriving normally</td><td>Nothing — this is the good state</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>LOS</strong></td><td>Red / blinking red</td><td>Loss of Signal — no light reaching the ONT</td><td>Reseat the fibre connector, power-cycle the ONT, then call 1218</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>LAN</strong></td><td>Blinking</td><td>Normal traffic to your router</td><td>Nothing</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>LAN</strong></td><td>Off</td><td>No connection to the router</td><td>Check the cable between the ONT and the router</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        The combination that matters: <strong>PON green + LOS off + LAN blinking</strong>
        is a healthy fibre link — if your internet is still slow, the problem is your
        router or Wi-Fi, not the fibre. <strong>LOS red</strong> is always a fibre
        problem, and no router restart will touch it.
      </p>

      <h2>Red LOS light: what to do</h2>
      <p>
        LOS means Loss of Signal — the ONT is receiving no light down the fibre. In order
        of likelihood: the fibre connector has worked loose, the cable is bent too
        tightly, the connector end is dirty, there's an outage, the fibre outside is cut,
        or the ONT has failed. Two things are safe to try yourself:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Reseat the fibre connector.</strong> The thin cable (usually with a green SC/APC connector) clips into the back of the ONT — push it in until it clicks. Check the cable isn't bent sharply or squashed behind furniture; fibre is glass and kinks easily.</li>
        <li><strong>Power-cycle the ONT.</strong> Switch it off for 30 seconds, then on, and wait for the lights to settle.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        If LOS is still red after both, stop — everything left is PTCL's cable or
        equipment. Call <strong>1218</strong>, say "red LOS light", and ask for a
        technician visit.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Safety:</strong> never look into the end of a fibre cable or an open ONT
        port. The light is infrared, so you can't see it — and it can damage your eye
        before you notice anything.
      </p>

      <h2>When the lights look fine but the internet is slow</h2>
      <p>
        Healthy lights with slow internet means the fault is past the modem: Wi-Fi, too
        many devices, congestion, or a slow line that still syncs. Run a
        <a href="/speed-test">speed test</a> over an Ethernet cable, then work through
        our <a href="/guides/troubleshoot-slow-internet">troubleshooter</a> — and on DSL,
        check your line stats (SNR margin and attenuation) in the modem settings.
      </p>

      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="faq-list" style="margin-top: 1rem;">
        {faqs.map((f) => (
          <details class="faq">
            <summary>{f.q}</summary>
            <p>{f.a}</p>
          </details>
        ))}
      </div>

      <div class="cta-band">
        <h2>Lights look healthy? Check the speed</h2>
        <p>Run a test to see what your line actually delivers.</p>
        <div class="cta-links">
          <a class="btn" href="/speed-test">Test My Speed →</a>
          <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/guides/ptcl-internet-slow">PTCL slow? Fix guide →</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
